<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geography (Ptolemy)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Geographia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Geography (Geographia) is a work by the second-century Greek scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy">Claudius Ptolemy</a>, describing the known world with coordinates. It records a joint expedition in which a Roman officer accompanied the Garamantian king south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>, which accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> cite as evidence of Garamantian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy.jpg" alt="Geography (Ptolemy)" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguel Servet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servet_Ptolomei_geographicae_enarrationis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ptolemy's Geographia</b>, drawing on Marinus of Tyre, records that a Roman named Julius Maternus accompanied the Garamantian king on an expedition south to the land of Agisymba in the late first century CE, which suggests a diplomatic relationship capable of producing formal military cooperation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>For more than five centuries the Sarmatians dominated the steppe between the Volga and the Danube, and Greek and Roman geographers called the whole region Sarmatia; Ptolemy's <b>Geography</b> divided it at the Don into European and Asian Sarmatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Auteini, or Autini, are listed in <b>Ptolemy's Geography</b> of about 150 on the west coast of Ireland, between the Nagnatae to the north and the Gangani (Ireland) to the south, roughly in modern Galway or Mayo.</blockquote><a href="/state/auteini">Auteini</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ptolemy's Geography</b> of about 150 lists the Brigantes in south-eastern Ireland, usually placed in the area of modern Wexford and Waterford, near the Coriondi and the Usdiae.</blockquote><a href="/state/brigantes-ireland">Brigantes (Ireland)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Caereni appear in <b>Ptolemy's Geography</b> of about 150 on the north-western mainland of Scotland, north of the Carnonacae, usually located in western Sutherland around Assynt and Kinlochbervie.</blockquote><a href="/state/caereni">Caereni</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carnonacae appear in <b>Ptolemy's Geography</b> of about 150 on the western coast of the Scottish Highlands, north of the Creones and south of the Caereni, roughly in modern Wester Ross.</blockquote><a href="/state/carnonacae">Carnonacae</a></li></ul></section>
